Add a VP9 backend and advertise it

VP9 decodes bit-exactly on the H713's video engine through GStreamer's
v4l2slvp9dec, but nothing could reach it through VA-API, so mpv and ffmpeg fell
back to libvpx on a quad Cortex-A53.

VA-API's VP9 interface was written for decoders that parse the headers in
hardware.  The picture parameters are a digest: segment quantisers arrive
already resolved to scale values, the loop-filter deltas are absent, and
nothing of the compressed header is passed at all.  The slice data, on the
other hand, is the whole frame with both headers in front.  V4L2's stateless
VP9 API wants the opposite -- the coded header fields, and the compressed
header's probability updates as deltas -- so this backend parses both headers
itself, following sections 6.2 and 6.3 of the VP9 specification: the
uncompressed header bit by bit, the compressed header through the boolean
decoder.  The deltas go across as V4L2 defines them: inv_map_table[] of the
decoded value for diff_update_prob(), the new probability itself for
update_mv_prob(), 0 for "no update" in both.

Two pieces of header state persist from one frame to the next unless a frame
changes or resets them -- the loop-filter deltas and the segmentation
features -- so they live in the context and are reset where the specification
calls setup_past_independence().

The two header sizes VA-API does carry are used as a cross-check: a parse that
disagrees with either has gone wrong somewhere before it, and every field read
after that point is garbage, so the frame is refused rather than decoded.

The reference surfaces, and so the timestamps that name them to the kernel,
come from VA-API as they should.  Profile 0 only: the kernel decodes 8-bit
4:2:0 VP9.

